  :adr/title "Which models run stably on a 16 GiB node, what stably means, and the two nodes that went dark measuring it"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-10"
  :adr/related ["adr-2609101100-a-node-dedicated-to-one-model"
                "adr-2609100100-one-model-per-node"]
  :adr/body
  "Owner instruction 2026-09-10: not gad -- find out which models run stably on
16 GiB, and record it. issachar was the bench.

## `stable` needed a definition before anything could be measured

Two failures the same day fixed what the word has to cover, and neither is the
model refusing to load:

- The weights are mmap'd. A model that does not fit does not fail -- it pages.
- A node earns its place in the pool by sending a heartbeat that ARRIVES. If
  inference starves the machine's network path the gateway drops the node while
  llama-server sits there answering /health.

So a config is `:stable` only when it loads, does not grow swap past the noise
floor, keeps an outbound request inside a quarter of the gateway's own 60 s
freshness line, and still has 8% of the machine free. The rule and its tests
are `murakumo.fleet.model-fit`; the probe is rendered from the same namespace.

## What was actually measured

    config              verdict     tok/s   free    outbound(load)   swap
    ornith9b  ctx 65536  stable      17.5   21.1%   594 ms           +0 MB
    ornith9b  ctx 131072 loaded       --      --    698 ms           --
    ornith9b  ctx 262144 loaded       --      --      --             --
    qwen27b   ctx 8192   (separate)    6.3    4.8%    --             -8 MB
    qwen27b   ctx 16384  NOT MEASURED
    qwen27b   ctx 32768  NOT MEASURED

The 9B at the context the fleet already serves it at is comfortably stable and
nearly three times faster than the 27B. It is the model this hardware is
shaped for. The 9B loaded at 131072 and at its full native 262144 in about two
seconds, but `loaded` is all that says: the ladder starts a server and serves
short requests, so it measures the ctx SETTING, not the ability to fill it.
Whether the KV cache is allocated eagerly at those settings is unmeasured --
the wired-memory readings that would have answered it were not captured before
the bench went dark. Do not read `loaded` as `has that much usable context`.

## The two nodes that went dark, and a causal claim withdrawn

issachar stopped answering ssh during the study. I reported that as a
measurement -- the model config taking the node off the network, the way a 27B
at ctx 32768 had taken levi off the network the day before. **That attribution
was wrong and is withdrawn.** Twenty minutes later joseph went dark the same
way, and nothing had been run on joseph.

What is actually true, measured from dan on the LAN and from the gateway:

    node      LAN ping   ssh :22   model :8092   tailnet    heartbeat
    issachar  OK         OPEN      shut          offline    3448 s stale
    joseph    OK         OPEN      shut          offline    1454 s stale
    dan/judah/benjamin/simeon -- all direct, all live

Both machines are alive. Only the tailnet path is gone, and both dark nodes are
the only two whose tailscale peering had fallen back to a DERP relay (`relay
\"tok\"`); the four healthy nodes all hold direct connections to the same NAT
address. The cause is NOT established. Port exhaustion is not it -- benjamin
has 52,015 sockets in TIME_WAIT with 1,521 ports free and is fine, while
issachar had 13,807 and is not.

## The finding that matters more than the ladder

**Neither dark node has a second way in, and the reason is structural.**
`ssh issachar` authenticates over the tailnet as Tailscale SSH; the same key
offered to the LAN sshd on 192.168.1.23 is refused, as is every key dan holds.
So port 22 is open, the machine is up, it is three metres away on the same
switch, and there is no credential that reaches it. This is the gap
`murakumo.access` was written to report, and it reported it honestly -- one
path -- while the fleet kept operating as though one path were enough.

That is the real answer to `how do we make this stable`, and it is upstream of
any model choice: **a node whose only access path is the overlay network is one
daemon away from needing a console, and two of six are there now.**

## Resume point

- `/tmp/model-fit-results.txt` on issachar holds the completed configs of the
  matrix, including whatever the 27B ladder recorded. macOS does not clear
  /private/tmp on reboot, so it should survive. Read it first.
- The 27B ladder above ctx 8192 is unmeasured, and so is the KV eagerness
  question for the 9B above 65536.
- Recovery of issachar and joseph needs a console or a LAN credential. Putting
  an authorized_key on each node's native sshd is the change that stops this
  recurring, and it is the same one levi has been waiting for."}]
